===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*men-|id=think}}
From {{inh|en|enm|coment}}, {{m|enm|comment}}, from {{der|en|fro|coment|t=commentary}}, from {{der|en|la-lat|commentum|t=comment, interpretation}}, from Classical {{der|en|la|commentum|t=invention, fabrication}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}

# A spoken or written [[remark]].
#: {{ux|en|I have no '''comment''' on that.}}
#: {{ux|en|Pay attention to the teacher's '''comments''' in the margin of your marked essay.}}
#* {{RQ:Chambers Younger Set|IX|passage=“A tight little craft,” was Austin’s invariable '''comment''' on the matron; and she looked it, always trim and trig and smooth of surface like a converted yacht cleared for action.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|date= 30 November 2015|page=12|title= Why Torture Doesn’t Work: The Neuroscience of Interrogation
|author= Shane O'Mara|publisher= w:Harvard University Press|url= https://books.google.co.nz/books?id=sZ9XCwAAQBAJ |isbn= 9780674743908 |passage=  Santorum, in a '''comment''' regarding Senator John McCain's repudiation of torture, stated, "He doesn't understand how enhanced interrogation works. I mean, you break somebody, and after they've broken they become cooperative" (Summers 2011).}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} The act of commenting.
#* {{RQ:Heller Catch-22|The Soldier in White|168|People bled to death like gentlemen in an operating room or expired without '''comment''' in an oxygen tent.}}
# {{senseid|en|rheme in linguistics}} {{lb|en|linguistics}} The part of a sentence that provides new information regarding the current [[theme]].
#: {{syn|en|rheme}}
# {{lb|en|programming}} A remark embedded in [[source code]] in such a way that it will be ignored by the [[compiler]] or [[interpreter]], typically to help people to understand the code.

